  "live_records": {
    "q-5358": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n+1}.$",
      "worked_solution": "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nThe first term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{1+1}=5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}\n$$\n\n \n\nand the second term is \n\n$$\n{a}_{2}=5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2+1}=5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{3}}{5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}}=\\frac{4}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{n+1} & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{5{(\\frac{4}{5})}^{2}}{1-\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{16}{5})}{(\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{5}\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
