    "q-1821": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "A card is drawn at random from a standard $52$-card deck. What is the probability of drawing a diamond card or a face card?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card deck has $4$ suits with $13$ cards in each suit. Diamonds is one of the suits. The face cards are the Jack, Queen, and King in each suit.*",
      "worked_solution": "Let's call $D$ the event \"a diamond card is selected\" and $F$ the event \"a face card is selected.\" \n\nThe required probability is $P(D\\cup F),$ which we can find using the addition law\n\n$$\nP(D\\cup F)=P(D)+P(F)-P(D\\cap F).\n$$\n\nLet's find $P(D)$ and $P(F).$\n\n- There are $13$ diamond cards in a standard deck. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D)=\\frac{13}{52}=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- There are $12$ face cards in a standard deck. So, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=\\frac{12}{52}=\\frac{3}{13}.\n$$\n\n- There are $3$ diamond face cards (the Jack, Queen, and King of diamonds). Therefore,\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap F)=\\frac{3}{52}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of the known information into the addition law, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D\\cup F) & =P(D)+P(F)-P(D\\cap F) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{13}-\\frac{3}{52} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
