    "q-29440": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14270828/assets/51a05d6b2fe04eb825b8048281b6d1e94b65df9f6f80c6e8965f9e414c5b360c.png)\n\nFind the inequality that describes the shaded region shown above.",
      "worked_solution": "First, we find the equation of the corresponding line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$\n\n- The line intersects the $y$-axis at $(0,1)$, so the $y$-intercept is $b=1.$\n\n- The line passes through the points $(0,1)$ and $(1,-1)$, so its slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m=\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}=\\frac{-1-1}{1-0}=\\frac{-2}{1}=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the line is $y=-2x+1.$\n\nFinally, we need to decide which inequality symbol to use. Since the line is dashed, we will use a strict inequality. Since the shaded region is *below* the line, the inequality refers to the $y$-values that are *less than* those on the line.\n\nSo, the corresponding inequality is\n\n$$\ny<-2x+1.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
