    "q-13550": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Find $K+L-(K+J)$, if\n\n$$\nJ=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,K=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}],\\,L=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we simplify the expression by distributing the subtraction over the matrices in the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}K+L-(K+J) & =K+L-K-J \\\\  & =\\cancel{K}+L-\\cancel{K}-J \\\\  & =L-J\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are left with a single subtraction. So, we subtract the corresponding entries of the two matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L-J & =[\\begin{aligned}6 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 6\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}6-5 & 6-2 \\\\ 6-2 & 6-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
