    "q-16463": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Given that $1-5\\text{i}=(p+\\text{i}q)(4+6\\text{i})$, calculate $p$ and $q.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we divide both sides of the equation by $4+6\\text{i}$ to isolate the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-5\\text{i} & =(p+\\text{i}q)(4+6\\text{i}) \\\\ \\frac{1-5\\text{i}}{4+6\\text{i}} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to simplify the fraction on the left-hand side, we multiply the numerator and denominator by the conjugate of the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(1-5\\text{i})(4-6\\text{i})}{(4+6\\text{i})(4-6\\text{i})} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{4-6\\text{i}-20\\text{i}+30{\\text{i}}^{2}}{{4}^{2}-(6\\text{i})^{2}} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{-26-26\\text{i}}{16+36} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ \\frac{-26-26\\text{i}}{52} & =p+\\text{i}q \\\\ -\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i} & =p+\\text{i}q\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can equate the real parts and the imaginary parts separately.\n\n- Equating the real parts, we get $-\\frac{1}{2}=p.$\n\n- Equating the imaginary parts, we get $-\\frac{1}{2}=q.$\n\nTherefore, $p=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and $q=-\\frac{1}{2}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
