    "q-134527": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Calculate the percentage error obtained when approximating the value of $(1.1)^{5}$ using the first three terms of the binomial expansion of $(1+x)^{5}$ in ascending powers of $x.$ Round your final percentage error to two decimal places.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that when $n$ is a positive integer, \n\n$$\n(1+x)^{n}=1+nx+\\frac{1}{2!}n(n-1){x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(1+x)}^{5} & =1+5x+\\frac{1}{2!}\\cdot 5\\cdot 4\\cdot {x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n} \\\\  & =1+5x+10{x}^{2}+⋯+{x}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, for small $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n{(1+x)}^{5}≈1+5x+10{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute $x=0.1$ into the above approximation of $(1+x)^{5}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(1.1)^{5} & =(1+0.1)^{5} \\\\  & ≈1+5(0.1)+10(0.1)^{2} \\\\  & =1+5(0.1)+10(0.01) \\\\  & =1+0.5+0.1 \\\\  & =1.6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using a calculator, we can compute the percentage error, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}%\\,error & =\\frac{\\text{amount of error}}{\\text{exact value}}\\times 100 \\\\  & =\\frac{|(1.1)^{5}-1.6|}{(1.1)^{5}}\\times 100 \\\\  & ≈0.65%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
