    "q-107737": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 15",
      "problem": "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{2}=5$ and ${a}_{9}=19,$ where ${a}_{2}$ and ${a}_{9}$ are the second and ninth terms of the series, respectively. What is the sum of the first $12$ terms of the series, expressed using sigma notation?",
      "worked_solution": "Since we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{9}-{a}_{2}}{9-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{19-5}{9-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{7} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs ${a}_{2}=5$, using the formula for the $n$th term, we can compute ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}+(2-1)(2) \\\\ 5 & ={a}_{1}+2 \\\\ {a}_{1} & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have ${a}_{1}=3$ and $d=2.$ Thus, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =3+(n-1)(2) \\\\  & =3+2n-2 \\\\  & =2n+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sum of the first $12$ terms can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(2n+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
