      "name": "Question 16",
      "problem": "The $3$rd term of a geometric sequence is $1$, and the $8$th term is $243.$ What is the first term of this sequence to exceed\n$3\\,001?$",
      "worked_solution": "Since we are given that ${a}_{3}=1$ and ${a}_{8}=243$, we can find the common ratio $r$ of the sequence, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{n-m} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}} \\\\ {r}^{8-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{8}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{5} & =\\frac{243}{1} \\\\ {r}^{5} & ={3}^{5} \\\\ r & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${a}_{3}=1$ and  $r=3$, we can use the $n$th term formula to solve for ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{3-1} \\\\ 1 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {3}^{2} \\\\ 1 & =9{a}_{1} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{9}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the $n$th term of the geometric sequence is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{-2}\\cdot {3}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={3}^{n-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want $n$th term to be greater than $3\\,001.$\nHence:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{n-3} & >3\\,001 \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡(3)^{n-3} & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,001) \\\\ (n-3)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & >\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,001) \\\\ n-3 & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,001)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)} \\\\ n & >\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,001)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEstimating the right-hand side using a calculator, we get:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3\\,001)}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}+3≈10.288\n$$\n\nTherefore, the $11$th term is the first term of the sequence that exceeds $3\\,001.$",
