    "q-46834": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 20",
      "problem": "Evaluate ${lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-4}{x-16}.$",
      "worked_solution": "If we attempt to evaluate the limit, we get an indeterminate form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-4}{x-16} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{16}-4}{16-16}=\\frac{0}{0}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to first simplify the limit. Since the radical is in the numerator, we can multiply the numerator and the denominator by the conjugate of the numerator. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-4}{x-16} & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{(\\sqrt{x}-4)(\\sqrt{x}+4)}{(x-16)(\\sqrt{x}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{{(\\sqrt{x})}^{2}-{4}^{2}}{(x-16)(\\sqrt{x}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{x-16}{(x-16)(\\sqrt{x}+4)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to 16}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{16}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
