    "q-65251": {
      "dom_id": "questionContainer",
      "name": "Question 23",
      "problem": "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we rewrite the second term using the definition of $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x.$ This gives:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nNext, we put both terms over a common denominator, and combine the two fractions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)} & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+({cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}+\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$",
